📊 The Three Panels of Wireshark
Your Wireshark window is divided into three sections:

Panel	What It Shows
Top (Packet List)	A list of all packets with Time, Source, Destination, Protocol, Info
Middle (Packet Details)	The structured breakdown of the selected packet
Bottom (Packet Bytes)	The raw data - the actual 1s and 0s in hexadecimal

### Understanding the Packet Bytes Pane -a Bottom where the data is just 1s and 0s

Column	What It Shows
a. 0000, 0010, 0020	The offset (position in the data stream)
b. 00 79 ad c7 40	The actual data in hexadecimal
c. The dots and text	The ASCII text version - what humans can read

#What each part means:
a. Left column (0000, 0010, etc.) = The offset (position in the data)
b. Middle columns (00 00 00 ...) = The actual data in hexadecimal
c. Right column (dots and letters) = The ASCII text representation

# Why the Packet Bytes Pane is Useful
- Deep troubleshooting: When something breaks, you can examine raw data to find the exact problem
- Security analysis: Spot malicious data hidden in packets
- Protocol understanding: See exactly what's being sent, byte by byte
- Forensics: Prove what data was actually transmitted




🔄 DNS Flow Analysis
The DNS Resolution Process (Example from My Capture)
Step 1: Client Questions
Packet #6058: 192.168.40.X → 192.168.40.1

Question: "What is the HTTPS record for pcdn.brave.com?"
Type: DNS Query (Standard query)
Packet #6059: 192.168.40.X → 192.168.40.1 (0.00015 seconds later!)

Question: "What is the A record (IP address) for pcdn.brave.com?"
Type: DNS Query (Standard query)

Step 2: Server Answers
Packet #6060: 192.168.40.1 → 192.168.40.X
Answer: "pcdn.brave.com is actually a CNAME alias for: [long-cloudflare-name].pacloudflare.com"
Type: DNS Response

Packet #6061: 192.168.40.1 → 192.168.40.X
Answer: "Here is the HTTPS service info for that Cloudflare domain"
Type: DNS Response


### Understanding Wireshark Arrows
a. Right arrow (→) = Request/question from your PC
b. Left arrow (←) = Response/answer from the server


### Key Learning: CNAME Records
CNAME = "Canonical Name" = an ALIAS
What happened: My PC asked for pcdn.brave.com, but the DNS server said:
"Actually, that's just an alias. The REAL name is this long Cloudflare name. Go ask that one for the IP."
Why this matters: This is common when websites use CDNs (Content Delivery Networks) like Cloudflare to speed up content delivery.


